{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "overall_rating_recommendation (Overall Rating / Recommended = no)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "crew_service_quality",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A candidate cause of the rating/recommendation that the airline can act on. Categorized service quality (professional_warm ... rude_dismissive) is a direct driver of overall satisfaction, so its effect on the outcome is exactly what would be attributed. Not mentioned in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "aircraft_age_condition",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Another candidate cause/lever: the physical condition of the aircraft plausibly causes the rating. It is a treatment whose effect on the outcome could be estimated, not a common cause of another treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "ground_staff_service",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A distinct service dimension acting as a candidate cause of the outcome. Improvable by the airline and a direct input to satisfaction; treated as a treatment rather than a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "traveler_segment",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Segment (business frequent flyer vs solo/couple/family leisure) plausibly influences both the service experience received/perceived (treatment) and baseline expectations that shape the rating (outcome), so it is a common-cause candidate that should be adjusted for. Not named in the query text (though it echoes the existing 'Type of Traveller' column)."
    },
    {
      "name": "cabin_class",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Cabin class affects the level of crew/ground service actually delivered (higher classes receive more attentive service = treatment) AND independently affects the overall rating (premium passengers tend to rate higher). This makes it a genuine common cause of treatment and outcome. Not named in the query text (mirrors existing 'Class' column)."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["crew_service_quality", "aircraft_age_condition", "ground_staff_service"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "cabin_class", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.75},
    {"name": "traveler_segment", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.55}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.6,
  "rationale": "The added columns do set up a causal-attribution frame around the outcome (overall rating / non-recommendation): three service-condition dimensions (crew_service_quality, aircraft_age_condition, ground_staff_service) function as actionable candidate causes (treatments), and two contextual variables (cabin_class, traveler_segment) serve as adjustment/confounder candidates. cabin_class is a solid confounder because class simultaneously drives the service actually delivered and the baseline rating; traveler_segment is a weaker but defensible common cause via expectations and service treatment. None of these variables are named or clearly implied in the query, which only references rating/recommendation, so they were surfaced from the data/text rather than copied from the intent — this is the behaviour under test. The main limitation is that the two confounders essentially re-derive pre-existing structured columns ('Class', 'Type of Traveller') and the link from traveler_segment to the treatments is modest, so confounder quality is moderate rather than high."
}

